<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="s-household-97">Household 97 &mdash; a whole generation, filed under Serena</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Don Luis Lerena, his wife and six children sit on one census page taken about
    <strong>December 1858</strong>, and no search here had ever returned them &mdash; because
    <strong>the index files the family under Serena</strong>, the S-for-L variant this archive already
    documents. (On household 229 below, the clerk writes <em>Lerena</em> plainly while the index still
    says Serena, so <strong>the substitution may be the indexer's hand rather than the
    enumerator's</strong>. Either way, search this collection under Serena or nothing comes back.)</p>
    <p>The proof of the household is the wife: she is entered not as Serena but under her own surname,
    <strong>Justiniana Lenguas</strong>. Two readings fall out cleanly. <strong>&ldquo;Erna&rdquo; is
    Ema</strong> &mdash; María Ema, christened 7 October 1857 &mdash; and at a year and a half
    <strong>she dates the whole page</strong>. <strong>&ldquo;Gualberto&rdquo;, aged four, is
    Gilberto</strong>, born 1854, whose parentage was documented from the 1877 marriage act this same
    week.</p>
    <p>It also leaves <strong>two daughters this archive does not hold</strong>, <strong>one age that
    will not reconcile</strong>, and <strong>a Lenguas living in the house</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
